{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788217746213,"sessionID":"ses_fa5eb3c6effeTntrFS9ccOqRTL","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"bash","callID":"chatcmpl-tool-8cdfeecf2c176efc","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"which erl erlc escript erl_call 2>&1; echo \"exit=$?\""},"output":"erl not found\nerlc not found\nescript not found\nerl_call not found\nexit=1\n","metadata":{"output":"erl not found\nerlc not found\nescript not found\nerl_call not found\nexit=1\n","exit":0,"truncated":false},"title":"which erl erlc escript erl_call 2>&1; echo \"exit=$?\"","time":{"start":1788217746210,"end":1788217746212}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"Now I have the full picture. The desk research file exists on the issue-163 branch (commit 9b9cec3) but not on my issue-170 branch. My task is the follow-up (issue-170): empirically verify the Erlang toolchain and the documented-semantics claims in the file (process/effect model, selective receive, pattern matching).\n\nThe task says:\n1. Read AGENTS.md (done via system prompt, but let me re-read key commit rules - actually it's in my instructions)\n2. Run gh issue view 170 (done)\n3. Read plans/erlang-target-research.md (doesn't exist on my branch; I read it from issue-163 branch)\n4. Attempt to empirically verify Erlang toolchain + documented-semantics claims\n5. If toolchain presence check is denied by sandbox → write addendum documenting the denial, note it confirms the same restriction hit by original research run (see opencode-rollout.md), and stop.\n6. If NOT denied → go further.\n\nThe task explicitly anticipates denial. The prior incident (2026-09-01, logged in opencode-rollout.md) confirms the `which erl escript erlc; erl -eval` denial. My brief says: \"If a toolchain presence check or running an erl/erlc binary is denied by the sandbox permission system, that is expected and is an infrastructure-level restriction, not something you can work around -- do NOT try alternate channels to run the binary anyway.\"\n\nSo I should ATTEMPT the toolchain presence check first, honestly. Let me try `which erl erlc escript` and maybe `erl -eval`. If denied, document and stop.\n\nLet me be careful — I must actually attempt the check to determine the outcome. Let me run the presence check.\n\nLet me first try `which erl erlc escript`.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_05a156230001o3f41A27IjbI9d","sessionID":"ses_fa5eb3c6effeTntrFS9ccOqRTL","messageID":"msg_05a154805001k8bDTR6qzN9sgN"}}
