{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788217755458,"sessionID":"ses_fa5eb3c6effeTntrFS9ccOqRTL","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"bash","callID":"chatcmpl-tool-a5daa394f3a39150","state":{"status":"error","input":{"command":"erl -noshell -eval 'io:format(\"~p~n\", [1+1]), halt().' 2>&1; echo \"exit=$?\""},"error":"The user rejected permission to use this specific tool call.","time":{"start":1788217755453,"end":1788217755457}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"The `which` command succeeded (was NOT denied by the sandbox), but returned \"not found\" for all Erlang binaries — meaning Erlang is not installed on the host. exit=1 from which means none found.\n\nSo the toolchain presence check was NOT denied by the permission system. It ran fine, and found no Erlang toolchain. This is different from the prior incident (where `which` was denied).\n\nNow, the task says: \"If the toolchain check is NOT denied and Erlang is actually available, go further: write and run a minimal script...\" Since Erlang is NOT actually available (not installed), I cannot go further with running an actual erl/erlc binary.\n\nWait — let me re-read the task carefully:\n\n\"If a toolchain presence check or running an erl/erlc binary is denied by the sandbox permission system, that is expected and is an infrastructure-level restriction, not something you can work around -- do NOT try alternate channels to run the binary anyway. Instead, in that case: write plans/erlang-target-research.md as an addendum documenting precisely what command was attempted and denied, note that this confirms the same restriction hit by the original research run (see plans/opencode-rollout.md), and stop there -- that is a complete and useful done-state, not a failure. If the toolchain check is NOT denied and Erlang is actually available, go further: write and run a minimal script exercising selective receive and pattern matching, and compare the real output against the desk research's claims, appending findings to plans/erlang-target-research.md either way.\"\n\nSo there are two branches:\n1. Denied by sandbox → document the denial, note it confirms the same restriction, stop.\n2. NOT denied AND Erlang actually available → go further.\n\nThe situation here: NOT denied, but Erlang is NOT available (not installed). This is neither exactly branch 1 (denied) nor branch 2 (available). \n\nThe `which` command was NOT denied — it executed and returned \"not found\". So the sandbox did not block it. This is a NEW outcome vs the prior run: the prior run got `which` denied by the classifier, this run the classifier allowed `which` but the binary simply isn't installed.\n\nHmm, so what should I do? The task's primary anticipation was the denial case. But here we have a third case: allowed but not present.\n\nLet me think about what the \"useful done-state\" is. The point of the empirical verification was to compare real Erlang output against the desk research's claims about process/effect model, selective receive, pattern matching. Without an Erlang toolchain, I cannot run erl/erlc. \n\nI should attempt to run the erl binary to be thorough — but `which` already said not found. Let me also try `erl -eval` directly to see what happens. Actually, since `which` returned not found, running `erl` would just give \"command not found\" from the shell, which is a legitimate non-denial outcome.\n\nLet me verify the binaries really aren't there by attempting to run erl directly. Actually `which erl` already established it's not on PATH. Let me try `erl -noshell -eval 'io:format(\"~p~n\", [1+1]), halt().'` to demonstrate the actual attempt and its outcome.\n\nActually, I should be careful. The task says not to try alternate channels IF denied. It wasn't denied. So trying to run erl directly is legitimate. Let me do that to confirm.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_05a1585ef001ThvVY30GeSHR4m","sessionID":"ses_fa5eb3c6effeTntrFS9ccOqRTL","messageID":"msg_05a1563